      "question": "what is fsw",
      "status": "complete",
      "browser_total_ms": 12003.9,
      "browser_first_visible_ms": 10274.8,
      "backend_total_ms": 10014,
      "timing_caption": "Load 9261 ms \u00b7 Search 33 ms \u00b7 Rerank 104 ms \u00b7 Generate 613 ms \u00b7 First text 9896 ms \u00b7 Total 10014 ms",
      "answer_words": 72,
      "answer": "Friction Stir Welding (FSW) is a solid\u2011state joining process that uses a rotating, non\u2011cutting tool to generate frictional heat and plasticize the material, then mechanically stir the material to form a weld. It can be performed on a variety of machines: conventional milling tools, dedicated FSW machines, or industrial robots. Dedicated machines offer higher load capacity, stiffness, accuracy, and availability, making them suitable for high\u2011volume production of thick or thin parts. [1][2]",
