    {
      "question": "How does it differ from the HAZ?",
      "status": "complete",
      "browser_total_ms": 1262.6,
      "browser_first_visible_ms": 785.5,
      "backend_total_ms": 614,
      "timing_caption": "Search 22 ms \u00b7 Rerank 92 ms \u00b7 Generate 500 ms \u00b7 First text 446 ms \u00b7 Total 614 ms",
      "answer_words": 100,
      "answer": "TMAZ (Thermo\u2011Mechanically Affected Zone)\nThe TMAZ is the region adjacent to the Stir Zone (SZ) that experiences both temperature rise and plastic strain during friction stir welding. Its microstructure is inhomogeneous because temperature and strain vary with distance from the SZ, leading to a hardness minimum in this zone. [2]\n\nDifference from the HAZ (Heat\u2011Affected Zone)\nThe HAZ lies further from the tool and is affected only by the heat generated during welding, without significant plastic deformation. Consequently, the HAZ typically shows a different microstructure and hardness profile compared to the TMAZ, which is plastically deformed but not recrystallized. [1]",
